hich we can do in the games industry  it's it's hard at times obviously but  but we are lucky to be able to do that  as compared to some folks who have to be  present you know at a certain facility  for their job  but yeah we've got people back in the  studio now in a hybrid situation and i  think i think it's working out pretty  well and yeah it's nice to see faces  collaborating and seeing each other and  getting excited about things and doing  kind of how i grew up making games which  is you go to someone's desk and look  over that and kind of ask questions  about it and learn in the process too  but hopefully we'll continue that way  for the remainder of the project and  beyond so i mean i know you obviously  came from bioware a lot of the team did  bioware obviously just  master class in storytelling so i was a  bit surprised earlier when you're like  yeah there is story but you know you're  really kind of doubling down on the  gameplay and stuff um but i guess is  that a chance for you to just  flex another another skill another  muscle yeah that's the hope lucy you  know like a bioware game is just such an  investment in storytelling and it  requires a huge team it requires lots of  very specialized skill sets and we never  we never could believe that we could  have uh the team size and the um  and the expertise to do all that so we  wanted to lower our ambitions a little  bit and create a great setting and  create an environment and we really  started to get the idea that it would be  fun to let players create their own  stories right and so how do you give  players more agency how do you give them  just a setting just a sandbox they can  have some fun in and do some things in  where it's unique and special to them  but at the same time they're gonna start  to tell their own stories and and that's  what really striving here to do so  there's a little little breadcrumbs  little little provocations if you will  of storytelling and characters you can  interact 